 HOW TO REVERSE: delete `tests/cli/test_list_commands_everywhere.py` and this paragraph.
+
+## DECISION F015 D1 — a plan edit is one locked transaction on the job record's stored task plan, revalidated by the planner's own checks, with the version and the whole-list edit log in the same write (2026-09-24)
+
+CONTEXT: T5_F015.md orders T001, a transactional editing backend operating on the STORED plan:
+load, apply one edit, revalidate with the same validator and schema the planner used, persist,
+regenerate `plan.md` and append the edit log, with no parallel plan representation. Measured at
+`fce49ce0`: the stored plan is the `task_plan` body of the job record
+`<data root>/jobs/<job id>/job.json`, which `do_sequence` writes as `TaskPlan.model_dump()` plus
+`_approval: pending` and `_normalization`; the scheduler reads `job.tasks`, which
+`map_task_plan_to_tasks` derives from that plan; `TaskPlan._validate_dag` refuses duplicate ids,
+unknown dependencies, cycles and more than 25 tasks, and no orphan check exists anywhere;
+`do_sequence` also refuses a plan whose mapped tasks fail `validate_deliverable_plan`; no plan
+revision counter exists, and `mission_compiler` names `_version` as the codebase's one versioning
+key; `task_granularity` has no public manual split or merge, and its private `_split_task`,
+`_merge_group` and `_rewire` carry the mechanics; nothing imports a plan-editing module.
+
+CHOSEN: (1) THE MODULE is `packages/orchestration/plan_editing.py`. `edit_plan(job_id, command,
+args, *, expected_version, actor, root)` is the one transaction, and `apply_edit(plan, command,
+args)` is the pure step it and the log replay share. (2) THE TRANSACTION holds a per-job advisory
+lock, `plan_edit.lock` in the job's directory, across load, check, apply, revalidate and ONE write
+of the job record, which carries the edited plan, `_version` bumped, the new log entry appended
+under `_edits`, and `job.tasks` regenerated by `map_task_plan_to_tasks`. `plan_v<n>.md` and
+`user_edited_plan.json` in the job's evidence export are regenerated after that write, from it. A
+refusal raises `PlanEditRefused` with a code before the write, so it changes nothing. (3)
+REVALIDATION is the planner's own: `TaskPlan.model_validate` (schema, DAG, cap), then
+`validate_deliverable_plan` over the mapped tasks. An ORPHAN is read as a dependency left pointing
+at a task an edit removed; the unknown-dependency check refuses it, and the delete and merge
+rewiring never leave one. (4) THE VERSION: a plan no edit has touched is version 1, under the
+`_version` key `mission_compiler` already names; every edit names the version it was made
+against, and a stale one is refused `version_conflict` with the current version. (5) THE WINDOW:
+`_approval` must read `pending` and the job must be `planned`; otherwise `plan_not_editable`
+names the state. (6) THE LOG ENTRY is `{version, ts, actor, command, args, before, after}`, where
+`before` and `after` are the whole task list, which is the before/after diff at its most literal
+and lets `replay_edits` rebuild the plan's evolution from the original. (7) THE EDITS: a delete
+hands each dependent the deleted task's own dependencies in its place; a merge fuses the named
+tasks in plan order with `_merge_group`, keeping the first id, and points the others' dependents
+at it; a split takes a partition of the task's acceptance indexes into at least two groups,
+builds the chain with `_split_task`, and its dependents wait for the chain's end, as the
+automatic split's do; an edit that changes nothing is refused. (8) Until T002 wires the door the
+module has no production importer, so `tests/test_no_orphan_modules.py` lists it in
+`ALLOWED_UNWIRED`, and T002 removes that line.
+
+ALTERNATIVES: the log as a JSONL file beside the record, rejected because the plan and its log
+would be two writes and a crash between them would part the evolution from the plan; a
+structural diff per edit, rejected as a second format the replay would have to interpret; a
+public manual split and merge in `task_granularity`, rejected because granularity's automatic
+pass is on the feature's Do-not-touch list and the private helpers already carry the mechanics,
+as `token_economy.py` and `job_apply.py` already import private names; reusing `_merge_blocker`,
+rejected because its first rule refuses a dependent of any member but the last, which a human's
+merge allows, and a merge that closes a cycle is refused by the DAG check anyway.
+
+HOW TO REVERSE: delete `packages/orchestration/plan_editing.py`,
+`tests/orchestration/test_plan_editing.py`, its `ALLOWED_UNWIRED` line and this paragraph.
